  "hints": [
    "Picking a value v once already removes every v - 1 and v + 1 from the board, so afterwards nothing stops you from picking the other copies of v.",
    "Group equal values together: the decision is only ever 'which distinct values do I take', and no two taken values may differ by exactly 1.",
    "Once the values are sorted, the valid predecessors of a value v are exactly a prefix of the list, so you can carry the best answer from two values back instead of rescanning."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "Compress the array into distinct values plus counts. Once value v is picked, all copies of v can be collected for v * count(v) points (each extra pick of v only re-destroys values that are already gone), and after picking v no element of value v - 1 or v + 1 can ever be scored. So the game collapses to: choose a subset of distinct values, no two chosen values differing by exactly 1, maximising the sum of v * count(v). The brute force sorts the distinct values and builds dp[i] = best score using values up to index i, where dp[i] = max(dp[i-1], earn(i) + max(dp[j] over all j < i with vals[i] - vals[j] >= 2)); the backward scan over j is O(k) per state. The optimal notices that 'all j with vals[j] <= vals[i] - 2' is just a prefix of the sorted values, so it never needs the inner scan: tabulating over the value range with dp[v] = max(dp[v-1], v * count[v] + dp[v-2]) and two rolling variables keeps the previous two answers.",
    "why_optimal": "The recurrence is a single forward pass over values with a constant amount of state, so O(n + max(nums)) time is optimal up to reading the input: every element must be counted at least once, and the value axis must be walked once. The brute force pays an extra factor of k (the number of distinct values) purely because it rescans earlier states to find the best compatible predecessor. max(nums) is at most 10000, so the value table is tiny, but note the table is sized by the maximum value, not by the array length, which is why the constraint on nums[i] matters.",
    "pitfalls": "Reading the rule as 'when I take v, every copy of v must also go' gives the wrong model; only the picked element is removed, so earn(v) = v * count(v) over all its copies. Adjacency must be compared on values and not on sorted positions: after dedup, two consecutive entry indices can be far apart in value, and a j with vals[i] - vals[j] >= 2 includes the immediate predecessor whenever it is not equal to vals[i] - 1. A single element array must return that element, an array of one repeated value must return value * count, and the DP must initialise dp[-1] and dp[-2] to 0 rather than to some sentinel."
  },
